uestion/id #uuid "b0863b6f-83a0-5fff-bd6b-d5b930ec7673" :question/math-academy-id "q-71396" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,0,1) & =2(-1)\\,i+2(0)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,i+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-2\\,i+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,0,1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71396/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71396"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection/3d3b665a60c364d063858357fd4b84dfae30d8b4528e0cbc9f31abc22d8c9ca0" :answer/id #uuid "863b837e-76b5-5969-8df3-62acae5a143c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection/69c4fdfbcb4bd135a7e8e62f861df6e94a09b5f9a536132d2ee50c378c60e545" :answer/id #uuid "5d4bf7de-a3b6-5196-baa3-a4db02fbd3a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\,i-\\,j-3\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection/248320d277020db110aa60e2cf277c956bad5e994acb90832b5924b4a078890d" :answer/id #uuid "43e370c9-8adc-508b-ae81-dec446f6bf9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection/b94a49820991126caf0360f732be46c0ebc1620085127128a89a857544b475cb" :answer/id #uuid "0104e92e-1a05-5108-acd9-de0e2116dea5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-4\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection/08f9e20a69a13be1f6e65a70dab5a587c7b3578dec4e072be6525b43c779c3d8" :answer/id #uuid "0c0836dd-7f8d-5691-b976-cc7b692ee4a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,i-\\,j-2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bdfb3d8-07a3-5325-9caf-d2bd2248ede2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-65168/selection/3d3b665a60c364d063858357fd4b84dfae30d8b4528e0cbc9f31abc22d8c9ca0" :answer-field/choices ["q-65168/selection/3d3b665a60c364d063858357fd4b84dfae30d8b4528e0cbc9f31abc22d8c9ca0" "q-65168/selection/69c4fdfbcb4bd135a7e8e62f861df6e94a09b5f9a536132d2ee50c378c60e545" "q-65168/selection/248320d277020db110aa60e2cf277c956bad5e994acb90832b5924b4a078890d" "q-65168/selection/b94a49820991126caf0360f732be46c0ebc1620085127128a89a857544b475cb" "q-65168/selection/08f9e20a69a13be1f6e65a70dab5a587c7b3578dec4e072be6525b43c779c3d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-65168" :question/id #uuid "1a91a6f8-cc47-5279-bcce-31ae7f779527" :question/math-academy-id "q-65168" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-1,1,-1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$  and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}z)\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+2xz)\\text{ }i+(2xy+{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+(2yz+{x}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-1,1,-1) & =((1)^{2}+2(-1)(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)(1)+(-1)^{2}))\\,j+(2(1)(-1)+(-1)^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i-1j-1\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-\\,j-\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-1,1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-65168/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65168"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection/947da72898dd46ce941403ebe7333a79145120cefb4cbf008e02fe37a359af49" :answer/id #uuid "240eb88e-c808-5b31-86d3-255676d89869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,i-2\\,j-k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection/7fc44339767192def569051958b4da8a6320146f6867510d397160a8bafc7f59" :answer/id #uuid "a598ecbe-78d2-55ba-b3c7-550be513ff8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,i-2\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection/07f4227f1fca5f7d6a41fe60d797c0c31d6108906270b35c6b6f12f706f549c9" :answer/id #uuid "02751068-ad51-5264-a97f-ed1d0ab4ebf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,i+j+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection/620152572f25d2e6461570ce4b61cd78d53815be5338059714b5504833a0afbb" :answer/id #uuid "bc802774-0b8b-589e-9209-b8912f2943d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "j-k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection/d5eaaf07f0505e9d5b231b8075c51e274f896039e198fc1181cc54f3b52b276e" :answer/id #uuid "00057f83-bb13-5625-8e40-bb4d96a6f0bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\,i+k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bb02f697-86e9-5804-abbd-791fbc7667f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131116/selection/7fc44339767192def569051958b4da8a6320146f6867510d397160a8bafc7f59" :answer-field/choices ["q-131116/selection/947da72898dd46ce941403ebe7333a79145120cefb4cbf008e02fe37a359af49" "q-131116/selection/7fc44339767192def569051958b4da8a6320146f6867510d397160a8bafc7f59" "q-131116/selection/07f4227f1fca5f7d6a41fe60d797c0c31d6108906270b35c6b6f12f706f549c9" "q-131116/selection/620152572f25d2e6461570ce4b61cd78d53815be5338059714b5504833a0afbb" "q-131116/selection/d5eaaf07f0505e9d5b231b8075c51e274f896039e198fc1181cc54f3b52b276e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131116" :question/id #uuid "da3be12b-b270-5258-9b1c-409842422d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-131116" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-1,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}+{z}^{2}{y}^{2}+2z)\\,k \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\text{ }i+2{z}^{2}y\\text{ }j+(2z{y}^{2}+2)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,-1) & =3(1)^{2}\\,i+2(-1)^{2}(-1)\\,j+(2(-1)(-1)^{2}+2)k \\\\  & =3\\,i-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $3\\,i-2\\,j$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-131116/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131116"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection/eeae9c4e49b60299c47d2bbb95a3a969b1017220a9d2028a4abc7de576a1a66e" :answer/id #uuid "4603d4c4-f0dc-50db-b1e8-6e7f9d0b518d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection/b1c0c31be971389d9e6af78d86e0a866f8ee719a82aeebbb9ee0b72c32ecc311" :answer/id #uuid "36587ced-e94f-583d-9084-b6d5de04bc55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection/fc3daad6c81a7071a42c43dc155ed90af7bfbba7764a756d6dafa883c4b9a188" :answer/id #uuid "f7a618f1-370f-58a0-9ad4-7625b95d0d34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection/9b366b0ee119218ac2418cc73aeca5ec920c69375ae11d14f3b1f2bbd531669d" :answer/id #uuid "53936b14-19a2-5bfa-af0e-04c4b4cd09f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection/123266cbfdeff9c56ec635826d2b8c4d9db55b39454e978209ee69a2464eb929" :answer/id #uuid "b661a14d-a933-5390-bb18-a029d3af2e59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "562cd7b1-d727-50b2-a1ea-c88c6cae0079" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71404/selection/9b366b0ee119218ac2418cc73aeca5ec920c69375ae11d14f3b1f2bbd531669d" :answer-field/choices ["q-71404/selection/eeae9c4e49b60299c47d2bbb95a3a969b1017220a9d2028a4abc7de576a1a66e" "q-71404/selection/b1c0c31be971389d9e6af78d86e0a866f8ee719a82aeebbb9ee0b72c32ecc311" "q-71404/selection/fc3daad6c81a7071a42c43dc155ed90af7bfbba7764a756d6dafa883c4b9a188" "q-71404/selection/9b366b0ee119218ac2418cc73aeca5ec920c69375ae11d14f3b1f2bbd531669d" "q-71404/selection/123266cbfdeff9c56ec635826d2b8c4d9db55b39454e978209ee69a2464eb929"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71404" :question/id #uuid "252876c9-7962-5371-8168-be2f8e38a47f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71404" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-71404/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71404"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection/3f6da73089804595b60dd5374a073a7724dd7e408d254faed18be4fbdf5515ab" :answer/id #uuid "21f39c96-e887-5a55-bed0-44bf0a59ad64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z-3}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection/21d66c4914b8459c4b4987c1ef34bcb83e55a198bdc90476b319f133d40ab680" :answer/id #uuid "e460517d-1510-58a0-8403-99223a713357" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection/934f1b01aec8094b1309997166ae68210cb37b07459538b21bca75dc2441a60c" :answer/id #uuid "d0fae3f4-e50e-5548-9c02-05ebc1598c29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=z-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection/d0cd969beedeeec2a36012fbac442cc75ac1aa765e2bc15a245e67337f9cb85b" :answer/id #uuid "fbc3c857-c4b3-5683-8199-1b81a7c465cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=z+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection/8873a4b0a2fa7af93ff2ddc7b8de5f39c9a35cbbae4769b01afb1be48926fd22" :answer/id #uuid "3d1e4e09-ca95-572e-b993-7475841863f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fba1b21-948c-5932-a272-2a0adb408b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-71411/selection/21d66c4914b8459c4b4987c1ef34bcb83e55a198bdc90476b319f133d40ab680" :answer-field/choices ["q-71411/selection/3f6da73089804595b60dd5374a073a7724dd7e408d254faed18be4fbdf5515ab" "q-71411/selection/21d66c4914b8459c4b4987c1ef34bcb83e55a198bdc90476b319f133d40ab680" "q-71411/selection/934f1b01aec8094b1309997166ae68210cb37b07459538b21bca75dc2441a60c" "q-71411/selection/d0cd969beedeeec2a36012fbac442cc75ac1aa765e2bc15a245e67337f9cb85b" "q-71411/selection/8873a4b0a2fa7af93ff2ddc7b8de5f39c9a35cbbae4769b01afb1be48926fd22"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-71411" :question/id #uuid "13503954-2456-5c13-9c01-1b77ab4bd258" :question/math-academy-id "q-71411" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $3{x}^{2}-xz=6-3xyz$ at the point $(1,0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-xz & =6-3xyz \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=6.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,-3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{x}^{2}-xz+3xyz)k \\\\  & =(6x-z+3yz)\\text{ }i+3xz\\text{ }j+(-x+3xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,-3) & =(6(1)-(-3)+3(0)(-3))\\text{ }i+3(1)(-3)\\text{ }j+(-1+3(1)(0))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =9\\,i-9\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨9,-9,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y-0}{-9}=\\frac{z-(-3)}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{9}=\\frac{y}{-9}=\\frac{z+3}{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-71411/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-71411"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection/4f8ab7ce5c7a40a9fce1a0363062c0304635942469650c7d34b7617d38d7159e" :answer/id #uuid "d79dd1bf-8d44-5ec7-8ea6-f4d87908b642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2=y+1=z-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection/5378a20b161ed9545351aca6da865f954af5ed39782b2944324d1dbedf7be602" :answer/id #uuid "aa7a2e5f-8044-58f8-bdca-b901aed23eb8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2=y+1=1-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection/41b46599c6ce2abb51bb80dbd02c4ac4a94f7aaa715bf80e82f7931e9d391279" :answer/id #uuid "cc835021-dea2-5a65-82ec-0221f2a80e24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2-x=y+1=1-z" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection/3dff372433c7102aa8877b2f12bcf28aae625b3541fafee198319db713ecb586" :answer/id #uuid "00c8ec18-b87f-5885-aa14-432139f277b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{y+1}{2}=\\frac{z-1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection/e5b68b11a0290faedab7bdc2683ba3ef0ac7b0cbcc64df632ecad27df6844c59" :answer/id #uuid "0059b6a0-a2d7-5fdb-b752-d8e015f173b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+1}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "549aa6f0-0020-509b-8885-63b47ef790e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131128/selection/41b46599c6ce2abb51bb80dbd02c4ac4a94f7aaa715bf80e82f7931e9d391279" :answer-field/choices ["q-131128/selection/4f8ab7ce5c7a40a9fce1a0363062c0304635942469650c7d34b7617d38d7159e" "q-131128/selection/5378a20b161ed9545351aca6da865f954af5ed39782b2944324d1dbedf7be602" "q-131128/selection/41b46599c6ce2abb51bb80dbd02c4ac4a94f7aaa715bf80e82f7931e9d391279" "q-131128/selection/3dff372433c7102aa8877b2f12bcf28aae625b3541fafee198319db713ecb586" "q-131128/selection/e5b68b11a0290faedab7bdc2683ba3ef0ac7b0cbcc64df632ecad27df6844c59"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131128" :question/id #uuid "c55cec54-efed-5c3b-a8a5-5b490ac1842f" :question/math-academy-id "q-131128" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}yz=-4$ at the point $(2,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}yz.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(2,-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}yz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}yz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}yz)k \\\\  & =2xyz\\text{ }i+{x}^{2}z\\text{ }j+{x}^{2}y\\,k \\\\ ∇f(2,-1,1) & =2(2)(-1)(1)\\text{ }i+(2)^{2}(1)\\text{ }j+(2)^{2}(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-4\\,i+4\\,j-4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-4,4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-4)}=\\frac{y-(-1)}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-4)} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(-1)}=\\frac{y+1}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ 2-x=y+1=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-131128/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131128"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection/71a0dc99a6880361fe995ba91fdf785bf54eb6ebabcf4433d1983771d22fc4e7" :answer/id #uuid "38bfb6ef-3482-5d6a-a1b0-e709ff632cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{y-2}{2}=z-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection/eb4502bb93f0f1845a5ccef99c31dfe37e9b677320c7bc60f336860a19b4c462" :answer/id #uuid "4b4fbd61-de16-537b-9bbc-326274088f89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-3}{2}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=z-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection/ae09811d38c2036e548c03f2e976e5f03464feabcd94b7ca0d1728169021776c" :answer/id #uuid "5333a7d8-85cc-5693-9601-3061dfb5c0e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x-1}{4}=2-y=z-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection/b9fd51802ee1a600a33e111f52c3e623276bb8a067c4490fc897bb5b55dc7ff1" :answer/id #uuid "3460f4fc-6ff3-5068-b7ef-fc30163f8d5d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-1=y-2=z-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection/252b534ba6dfaa19377863fd7f791f04d42ccf534ba545559af9c730b07988b9" :answer/id #uuid "5ad2e308-0172-5965-bc1e-c9c18b0b865e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6c54c0-de64-5467-8fc5-cd3d04fffd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131130/selection/252b534ba6dfaa19377863fd7f791f04d42ccf534ba545559af9c730b07988b9" :answer-field/choices ["q-131130/selection/71a0dc99a6880361fe995ba91fdf785bf54eb6ebabcf4433d1983771d22fc4e7" "q-131130/selection/eb4502bb93f0f1845a5ccef99c31dfe37e9b677320c7bc60f336860a19b4c462" "q-131130/selection/ae09811d38c2036e548c03f2e976e5f03464feabcd94b7ca0d1728169021776c" "q-131130/selection/b9fd51802ee1a600a33e111f52c3e623276bb8a067c4490fc897bb5b55dc7ff1" "q-131130/selection/252b534ba6dfaa19377863fd7f791f04d42ccf534ba545559af9c730b07988b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131130" :question/id #uuid "48fd28c2-a303-5fa6-8022-75364769fa65" :question/math-academy-id "q-131130" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $x={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}$ at the point $(3,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={y}^{2}-{z}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(3,2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(3,2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(3,2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}-{z}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =-i+2y\\,j-2z\\,k \\\\ ∇f(3,2,1) & =-i+2(2)\\,j-2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-i+4\\,j-2\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,4,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{(-1)}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ \\frac{x-3}{1}=\\frac{y-2}{-4}=\\frac{z-1}{2} \\\\ x-3=\\frac{2-y}{4}=\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-131130/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131130"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection/237ff2b8773e9a3c0e48463d121a0e2f5eff28902813d24bd263e07fc6daa9a4" :answer/id #uuid "bfc8ef8e-31fe-52e1-b303-8f9fbc3cc657" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,-1,1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection/060834b165782c798c8b424ce06b5df82e2f7adcfe7340dedad871e3f6d9400f" :answer/id #uuid "c2a515a0-1ec1-5aba-92e6-0b0178195eb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection/cc9d4c2b59e2b7f1a50e38454ac1db95424f02e1fdd3384a531338263ad8011a" :answer/id #uuid "ffaaae16-ece7-5a63-8545-bc4a5d1ea433" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection/9861a2d6a2eeb13763933ccfcf7157bd7887552f6d963e3dc1a884405c112a25" :answer/id #uuid "fa458864-16e0-5032-88db-1a9e022fe159" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,1,-1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection/b3adc1ae8c17466b88212dc5810e6111b160604901c2134b6ef8c629d4e791e4" :answer/id #uuid "e5764784-0edd-52ca-be9e-feeb012215a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,1,-1⟩+t\\,⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6c49b1c-dab2-5442-af31-e47d008ee921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131127/selection/cc9d4c2b59e2b7f1a50e38454ac1db95424f02e1fdd3384a531338263ad8011a" :answer-field/choices ["q-131127/selection/237ff2b8773e9a3c0e48463d121a0e2f5eff28902813d24bd263e07fc6daa9a4" "q-131127/selection/060834b165782c798c8b424ce06b5df82e2f7adcfe7340dedad871e3f6d9400f" "q-131127/selection/cc9d4c2b59e2b7f1a50e38454ac1db95424f02e1fdd3384a531338263ad8011a" "q-131127/selection/9861a2d6a2eeb13763933ccfcf7157bd7887552f6d963e3dc1a884405c112a25" "q-131127/selection/b3adc1ae8c17466b88212dc5810e6111b160604901c2134b6ef8c629d4e791e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131127" :question/id #uuid "99fa8446-b5a9-5ac5-a425-7d1aaeb62335" :question/math-academy-id "q-131127" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ at the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}z+y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}z-y)k \\\\  & =(2xy{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2xz)i+({x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-1)j+(2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2})k \\\\ ∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1) & =(2(-1)(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2(-1)(1))i+((-1)^{2}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2}\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(-1)^{2})k \\\\  & =(-\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2)i+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)j+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-1)k \\\\  & =2\\,i-j+k \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ and passes through the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,∇f(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)=⟨-1,\\frac{\\pi }{2},1⟩+t\\,⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-131127/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131127"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection/66582114ea4dc5c57ece8fbdf62323621e160c2d524277c75867d4238ceaea82" :answer/id #uuid "56c21728-26d3-53c5-bae4-422f40392860" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨-2,5,-3⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection/285f87c5e5288bc28ad4e36b6588c1dbe051e359e9e8e667c0e102f31668da32" :answer/id #uuid "de7d97b5-c531-5706-9be5-b9f9a1118fcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection/10adcabb35fe38f661ffb14d840b1ef3e4da7435f29f336159d9f1931d6125ab" :answer/id #uuid "5eeab54b-0461-5b37-b48c-3b3ae249f7fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨-2,5,-3⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection/0f90bf7c378d49e018bc2d9cef512982cc361cea59b28fd34f45feaf82821620" :answer/id #uuid "a015312e-e549-5876-981e-21b6923273cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨4,5,-5⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection/1ef1e00b758549d54fdb42aac24853f687367c698ef4e683a482422ad55822f5" :answer/id #uuid "7dc82a54-6770-583d-a42a-ec8448aa358b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨2,3,-5⟩+t\\,⟨1,-1,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "225ef13f-bb07-530a-98c0-5c877c04cd97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-131126/selection/285f87c5e5288bc28ad4e36b6588c1dbe051e359e9e8e667c0e102f31668da32" :answer-field/choices ["q-131126/selection/66582114ea4dc5c57ece8fbdf62323621e160c2d524277c75867d4238ceaea82" "q-131126/selection/285f87c5e5288bc28ad4e36b6588c1dbe051e359e9e8e667c0e102f31668da32" "q-131126/selection/10adcabb35fe38f661ffb14d840b1ef3e4da7435f29f336159d9f1931d6125ab" "q-131126/selection/0f90bf7c378d49e018bc2d9cef512982cc361cea59b28fd34f45feaf82821620" "q-131126/selection/1ef1e00b758549d54fdb42aac24853f687367c698ef4e683a482422ad55822f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-131126" :question/id #uuid "aeaadd89-dc14-54c2-bff3-8d905f0aebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-131126" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x=5$ at the point $(1,-1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=5.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,-1,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,-1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}z+{z}^{2}x)k \\\\  & =(2xy+{z}^{2})\\,i+({x}^{2}+2yz)\\,j+({y}^{2}+2zx)\\,k \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,2) & =(2(1)(-1)+(2)^{2})\\text{ }i+((1)^{2}+2(-1)(2))\\text{ }j+((-1)^{2}+2(2)(1))\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\  & =⟨2,-3,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(1,-1,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,∇f(1,-1,2)=⟨1,-1,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-3,5⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-131126/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7d6557-ec20-5a40-b799-1a6a36cbe9e6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-131126"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3432"] :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2y^2 +x$ that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1, 1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y) = (1,1)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(1,1).$  \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y) = (1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+0+1)\\,i+(0+4y+0)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+1)\\,i+4y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(1,1) & =(2(1)+1)\\,i+4(1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we want a unit normal vector, we normalize $\\nabla f(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(1,1)}{‖∇f(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{3\\,i+4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3433"] :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1.$ Find an equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(0, 4).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16} - 1,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(0,4).$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-1)j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }i+\\frac{(4)}{8}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle v_x, v_y \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{x-{x}_{0}}=\\frac{{v}_{y}}{{v}_{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nor, equivalently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{y}(x-{x}_{0}) & ={v}_{x}(y-{y}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(0,4)= \\left\\langle 0, \\:\\dfrac12 \\right\\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(x-0) & =0(y-4) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the equation of the normal line to the ellipse $\\dfrac{x^2}9 + \\dfrac {y^2}{16}=1$ at the point $(0,4)$ is $x=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3434"] :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z) = 2x^2 - xy - z^2,$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)$ can be found by computing \n$\\nabla f(2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$   \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}-xy-{z}^{2})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j-2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,3,\\sqrt{2}) & =(4\\cdot 2-3)\\text{ }i-2\\text{ }j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =5i-2j-2\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\mathbf i  - 2 \\mathbf j - 2 \\sqrt 2\\ \\mathbf k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z) = (2, 3, \\sqrt 2).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3435"] :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the surface $z = \\ln (x^2 - xy)$ at the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To find the equation of the normal line, we first find the equation of a normal vector, and then write the equation of the line that is parallel to the normal vector.\n\nTo obtain a normal vector, we write our curve as a level curve and then compute the gradient. Bringing all the terms of our equation to one side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy) \\\\ 0 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z) = \\ln (x^2 - xy) - z,$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $\\left(2, 1, \\ln 2 \\right)$ can be found by computing $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2){:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}-xy)-z)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2x-y}{{x}^{2}-xy})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{y-x})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(2,1,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =(\\frac{2\\cdot 2-1}{{2}^{2}-2\\cdot 1})\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{1-2})\\text{ }j-\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}i-\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, remember that the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\mathbf v = \\langle v_x, v_y, v_z \\rangle$ and passes through the point $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $\\nabla f(2,1,\\ln 2)= \\left\\langle  \\dfrac {3} {2}, -1, -1\\right  \\rangle,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})}=\\frac{y-1}{-1}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-1} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3}=\\frac{y-1}{-2}=\\frac{z-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
